			<div class="content" id="post-content-3914860">
									

<p>I strongly dislike this.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The subject is Caucasian in descent, possessing blonde hair, blue eyes, and pale skin.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You call the skip the subject and not the anomaly or the object. (I'm seeing that error more and more these days &#8212; did we stop trying to enforce that or something?) And I keep trying to figure out why it's so hard to refrain from saying &quot;why did you describe an <a href="http://www.sahistory.org.za/article/human-rights-issues-during-and-after-world-war-ii-focus-nazi-germany-grade-12-0">Aryan</a>&quot; &#8212; it's not like people aren't allowed to look like that, lol &#8212; so I think it's because &quot;pale skin&quot; is wholly unnecessary when you already specify race.</p>
<p>The whole thing about letting a D-class stay with her was terrifying. I like writing the Foundation as 'the nice side of cold' too but this is too far IMO; the dude's a <em>violent felon.</em> What the hell is the Ethics Committee <em>on</em> in this? The Foundation's job isn't to make sure she's got someone to cuddle with, it's to stop her from hurting people/disrupting normality and keep her safe from herself in the process.</p>
<p>And he ended up hurting her, which just makes their move look dumber from a narrative standpoint regardless of the fact that he did it because he was in the cult. Like, the fact that chances led to him being from the cult just proves that they shouldn't have done it. It's not a good way to introduce the notion of letting someone stay with a skip.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Before security could intervene, Junior Researcher Davis had entered the cell and engaged D-36336 in an attempt to divert his attention from SCP-3873. Agent White discharged his firearm at D-36336, grazing JR Davis's left arm, penetrating D-36336's collarbone, and exiting through the shoulderblade. At which point, <strong>D-36336 desisted before being approached, shot again, and killed by Agent White.</strong> SCP-3873 was examined by medical staff and treated for minor blunt force trauma.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So was Agent White reprimanded for killing a subject who surrendered? Why was he using lethal force in a containment chamber with only one normal human perpetrator anyway? Even civilian tasers can have a range of 25 feet and this room cannot possibly be that big. The Foundation values lives. Even D-class ones, because they need living human bodies to use. Killing someone like this is a waste of personnel. If his behavior is too shit for anything else, he can be amnesticized and assigned to something that doesn't require interacting with a sapient entity.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>SCP-3873:</strong> That's it, huh? Just a report? Paperwork? I am a person, doctor! I am a human, just like you!</p>
<p><em>Dr. Porter pauses.</em></p>
<p><strong>Dr. Porter:</strong> I know, Riley. We all know. There are many more like you. Many more who continue to suffer fates that we wish we could help them out of. But we can't. That's a reality that most of us have come to accept. The multiverse is a cruel place. I'm sorry. We have to get back to work now.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The 'I'm sapient waah'/'we don't care I'm doing my job' skip/employee exchange trope is not unique in general or in this application.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It was concluded that the effect had somehow passed onto him when it was neutralized from the initial SCP-3873 host.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nothing has been established to indicate that this is a thing that can happen. Dropping it as a surprise at the end just leaves more unanswered questions, and not in a good way.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It has been determined that Dr. Porter contracted the anomaly by sheer chance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Locating the current host of the anomaly is a Delta-level priority. I have assigned Mobile Task Force Iota-10 to monitor governmental and judicial chatter to locate the current host and place them into containment. We have a whole world to search. We will not make this mistake again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's cool, but no focus is put on these elements at all until here. The story is about a girl being miserable while men make her miserable, and then the narrative tries to switch to the wide-scale look at the cult's impact on the world instead. The end result is split focus.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Between these nitpicks and some social issue elements I'll refrain from addressing in depth because they're subjective, this whole thing is just uncomfortable and clashes with my headcanon egregiously.</p>

							</div>

								
